"Beginning this September, Dryad adopted a subset of CRediT to better define author contributions to the datasets we publish.&nbsp;

The full Contributor Role Taxonomy, more commonly known as CRediT, establishes 14 author roles spanning the research process from ‘conceptualization’ to ‘writing – review, and editing’ of journal articles. It’s designed to give full and fair credit to everyone involved in a research investigation regardless of seniority, and at the same time limit honorary or gift authorship.

Dryad now supports a subset of 9 author roles which most directly apply to the creation, curation, and publication of datasets, in keeping with recommendations by the Generalist Repository Ecosystem Initiative for generalist repositories."

"NIH is fully committed to ensuring that the American people have speedy access to the research results which they helped support.

NIH unequivocally asserts its right to post Author Accepted Manuscripts in PubMed Central at the time of publication. NIH’s right is independent from any publisher agreement. NIH’s Public Access Policy has a free compliance pathway for authors through PubMed Central. &nbsp;

Rapid public access to the fruits of NIH-supported research is essential to fulfilling the promise of improved health. NIH’s Public Access Policy brings that promise one step closer to reality. "

Written by Hannah Hillen, Metadata &amp; Publisher Outreach Specialist, Thoth Open Metadata 10 September 2026

We’re delighted that Thoth Open Metadata has been selected as a finalist for the 2026 ALPSP Award for Innovation in Publishing.&nbsp;

It’s particularly exciting to return to the ALPSP Awards this year because a great deal has changed at Thoth over the past twelve months. In April, we launched Thoth 1.0, bringing together the different strands of our work into an expanded suite of services for publishers. At the same time, the principle at the heart of Thoth remains unchanged: making it easier for publishers to create high-quality open metadata and use it to get their books discovered, accessed and preserved.&nbsp;

